      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number of coins that satisfies the given conditions:\n\n1. When the coins are divided among six people, four coins are left over.\n2. When the coins are divided among five people, three coins are left over.\n\nLet \\( N \\) be the number of coins in the box. Then we can write the following congruences:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive integer \\( N \\) that satisfies both congruences. First, let's express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[ k = 5m + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[ N = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nSo, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[ N = 30m + 28 \\]\nThe smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), which gives:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nNow, we need to determine the number of coins left when \\( N = 28 \\) is divided among seven people:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\]\nSo, when 28 coins are divided among seven people, the remainder is 0. Therefore, the number of coins left is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
