      "text": "1. **Understanding the Problem Statement:**\n   - We need to find the smallest number of coins \\( N \\) such that:\n     \\[\n     N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Setting Up Congruences:**\n   - The first congruence is:\n     \\[\n     N = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n     \\]\n   - The second congruence is:\n     \\[\n     N = 5m + 3 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n     \\]\n\n3. **Expressing \\( N \\):**\n   - Substitute \\( N = 6k + 4 \\) into the second congruence:\n     \\[\n     6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n     Simplifying this, we get:\n     \\[\n     6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n     Therefore, we can write:\n     \\[\n     k = 5n + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } n\n     \\]\n\n4. **Finding \\( N \\):**\n   - Substitute \\( k = 5n + 4 \\) back into \\( N = 6k + 4 \\):\n     \\[\n     N = 6(5n + 4) + 4\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     N = 30n + 24 + 4\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     N = 30n + 28\n     \\]\n\n5. **Finding the Smallest Positive \\( N \\):**\n   - The smallest positive \\( N \\) occurs when \\( n = 0 \\):\n     \\[\n     N = 30 \\cdot 0 + 28 = 28\n     \\]\n\n6. **Dividing by 7 and Finding the Remainder:**\n   - Now, we need to find the remainder when \\( N = 28 \\) is divided by 7:\n     \\[\n     28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of } 0\n     \\]\n     Therefore, the remainder is:\n     \\[\n     28 \\mod 7 = 0\n     \\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
